Q: Return value type of class member accessor function to provide access to vector of unique_ptrs A: <p>If the vector of <code>Location</code> objects is set only once (say at construction), you have also the possibility to sort it with <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/algorithm/ranges/sort.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::ranges::sort</code></a> with the help of a projection (here a lambda that returns the <code>label</code> field of a <code>Location</code> object) :</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class LocationDataBase {
 public:
    LocationDataBase (std::vector&lt;Location&gt;&amp;&amp; data) : location_data_(data) {
        std::ranges::sort (location_data_, {}, [] (auto&amp;&amp; e) { return e.label; } );
    };
 private:
    std::vector&lt;Location&gt; location_data_;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Since the vector is sorted, you can then have an efficient search of the first and last occurrence of an item as follows; note how we use again a projection on the <code>label</code> field</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::span&lt;Location&gt; GetObjectsAtLocation (std::string const&amp; label) {
    auto proj = [] (auto&amp;&amp; e) { return e.label; };
    return std::span&lt;Location&gt; (
        std::ranges::lower_bound(location_data_, label, {}, proj),
        std::ranges::upper_bound(location_data_, label, {}, proj)
    );
}
</code></pre>
<p>Note how one uses <code>std::ranges::lower_bound</code> and <code>std::ranges::upper_bound</code> and provides their result to build a <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/container/span.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>std::span</code></a> object; using a <code>std::span</code> involves no copy of <code>Location</code> objects since a <code>std::span</code> is a view on an already existing object (a <code>std::vector</code> in that use case).</p>
<p>By doing so, you need only one container (<code>location_data_</code>) that provides efficient search of <code>Location</code> objects given a label. This proposal requires C++23 because of usage of some <code>std::ranges</code> features.</p>
<p>This is just a proposal that allows to keep only one container. However, the way the lookup is done needs two lookups instead of one. It could be improved though to a certain extent and depending to your actual use case, it might be not too costly.</p>
<p><a href="https://godbolt.org/z/4xczT1s6x" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Demo</a></p>

Q: How to rewrite target url within an AWS ALB Ingress Controller? A: <p><strong>UPDATE:</strong> You can now use the <code>alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/transforms.${transforms-name}</code> annotations to do so</p>
<pre class="lang-yaml prettyprint-override"><code>alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/transforms.my-service: &gt;
    [
        {
            &quot;type&quot;: &quot;url-rewrite&quot;,
            &quot;urlRewriteConfig&quot;: {
                &quot;rewrites&quot;: [
                    {
                        &quot;regex&quot;: &quot;^\\/api\\/(.+)$&quot;,
                        &quot;replace&quot;: &quot;/$1&quot;
                    }
                ]
            }
        }
    ]
</code></pre>
<p>See: <a href="https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/aws-load-balancer-controller/latest/guide/ingress/annotations/#transforms" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://kubernetes-sigs.github.io/aws-load-balancer-controller/latest/guide/ingress/annotations/#transforms</a></p>
